# Sustainable Fashion Research Plan Revision<br>
<br>
## Introduction<br>
<br>
The fashion industry is one of the largest polluters globally, with significant environmental and social impacts. This research plan aims to explore sustainable fashion practices and materials, with a focus on upcycled textiles, recycled polyester, and organic cotton.<br>
<br>
## Upcycled Textiles<br>
<br>
Upcycled textiles are made from post-consumer waste, reducing the need for virgin materials and minimizing waste. Examples include:<br>
<br>
* Denim recycling programs<br>
* Old clothing turned into new products (e.g., bags, accessories)<br>
<br>
## Recycled Polyester<br>
<br>
Recycled polyester is a sustainable alternative to traditional polyester, using recycled plastic bottles as raw material. Benefits include:<br>
	+ Reduced greenhouse gas emissions<br>
	+ Conserved water and energy resources<br>
	+ Minimized waste<br>
<br>
## Organic Cotton<br>
<br>
Organic cotton is grown without synthetic pesticides or fertilizers, promoting soil health and biodiversity. Advantages include:<br>
<br>
	+ Improved crop yields and reduced pesticide use<br>
	+ Enhanced ecosystem services (e.g., pollination, pest control)<br>
	+ Potential for increased water retention and reduced erosion<br>
<br>
## Conclusion<br>
<br>
This research plan provides a foundation for exploring sustainable fashion practices and materials. Future studies should investigate the feasibility of implementing these strategies in the fashion industry.<br>
<br>
# References<br>
<br>
* EcoCycle: Sustainable Fashion Guide<br>
* Green Living: Upcycled Textiles 101<br>
